// Software Engineering, Unit II: agile development and requirements engineering.
// Follows the Unit 2 notes and slides (Pressman for agility and requirements engineering; Jalote for the SRS).
export default {
  'se.u2.agility': {
    oneLine: 'Agility is the ability to respond to change quickly and effectively; an agile process is adaptive and incremental, delivers working software in short iterations with the customer on the team, and is guided by the four values and twelve principles of the Agile Manifesto.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Agility', text: 'The **ability to respond effectively to change**: change in the software being built, in team members, in new technology, and in anything that affects the product. Agility also means **effective communication among all stakeholders**, the customer on the team, a well-organised team in control of its work, and **rapid, incremental delivery** of working software.' },
      { def: 'Agile process', text: 'A process that addresses three key assumptions: it is difficult to predict in advance **which requirements will persist and which will change**; **design and construction are interleaved**; and **analysis, design, construction and testing are not as predictable** as we would like. So an agile process must be **adaptable**, adapting **incrementally**, which needs **customer feedback** on operational prototypes or increments delivered in short periods.' },
      { h: 'The Agile Manifesto (2001)' },
      { table: { head: ['We value…', '…over'], rows: [['**Individuals and interactions**', 'processes and tools'], ['**Working software**', 'comprehensive documentation'], ['**Customer collaboration**', 'contract negotiation'], ['**Responding to change**', 'following a plan']] } },
      { h: 'Agility and the cost of change' },
      { img: 'se-cost-of-change' },
      'In conventional development the cost of change rises steeply as the project progresses. A well-designed agile process **flattens the curve**, because incremental delivery, continuous unit testing and pair programming let a team accommodate changes late in a project without dramatic cost.',
      { h: 'The twelve agility principles' },
      { ol: ['Satisfy the customer through **early and continuous delivery** of valuable software.', '**Welcome changing requirements**, even late in development.', 'Deliver working software **frequently**, from a couple of weeks to a couple of months.', 'Business people and developers **work together daily**.', 'Build projects around **motivated individuals**; trust them.', '**Face-to-face conversation** is the most efficient way to convey information.', '**Working software is the primary measure of progress**.', 'Promote **sustainable development**: a constant pace indefinitely.', 'Continuous attention to **technical excellence and good design**.', '**Simplicity**: maximising the amount of work not done.', 'The best architectures, requirements and designs emerge from **self-organising teams**.', 'At regular intervals the team **reflects** on how to become more effective and adjusts.'] },
      { h: 'Human factors of an agile team' },
      'Competence, common focus, collaboration, decision-making ability, fuzzy problem-solving ability, mutual trust and respect, and self-organisation.',
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Agile', items: ['Change is expected and welcomed', 'Small increments every few weeks', 'Customer on the team throughout', 'Light documentation; working software measures progress'] },
        { title: 'Plan-driven (conventional)', items: ['Change is controlled and costly', 'One delivery at the end', 'Customer at the start and end', 'Heavy documentation; plans measure progress'] },
      ] },
    ],
    terms: [['Agility', 'Ability to respond effectively to change.'], ['Agile Manifesto', 'Four values and twelve principles (2001).'], ['Self-organising team', 'Team that decides how to do its own work.']],
    exam: 'Define agility and an agile process (three assumptions), give the four manifesto values, draw the cost-of-change curve, list the principles, and compare agile with plan-driven development.',
    revise: ['Individuals, working software, collaboration, responding to change', 'Adaptive + incremental + customer feedback', 'Agile flattens the cost-of-change curve'],
  },

  'se.u2.xp': {
    oneLine: 'Extreme Programming (XP), by Kent Beck, is the most widely used agile process: it works through planning (user stories), design (keep it simple, CRC cards, spike solutions), coding (test-first, pair programming, continuous integration) and testing (daily unit tests, customer acceptance tests), guided by five values.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Extreme Programming (XP)', text: 'An agile software development method that uses an **object-oriented approach** as its preferred paradigm and encompasses a set of rules and practices within four framework activities: **planning, design, coding and testing**. It is built on five values: **communication, simplicity, feedback, courage and respect**.' },
      { img: 'se-xp' },
      { h: '1. Planning (the planning game)' },
      { ul: ['Begins with **listening**: the customer writes **user stories** (short descriptions of required features) on index cards and assigns each a **value** (priority).', 'The team estimates each story\'s **cost** in development weeks; stories over three weeks are split.', 'Customer and team decide which stories go into the next **release** (all at once, highest value first, or riskiest first).', 'After the first release, **project velocity** (stories implemented in the first release) is used to estimate delivery dates and plan later releases.'] },
      { h: '2. Design' },
      { ul: ['**Keep it simple (KIS)**: a simple design is always preferred over a complex one; design only what is needed now.', '**CRC cards** (class–responsibility–collaborator) identify and organise the object-oriented classes.', 'For a difficult design problem, build a **spike solution**: an immediate operational prototype of that part, to lower risk.', '**Refactoring**: improving the internal structure of the code without changing its external behaviour; design happens continuously.'] },
      { h: '3. Coding' },
      { ul: ['**Test-first**: write the unit tests for a story before writing the code.', '**Pair programming**: two people work together at one workstation; real-time problem solving and quality assurance.', '**Continuous integration**: pairs integrate their code with the others\' work several times a day, which avoids compatibility problems and gives early warning.'] },
      { h: '4. Testing' },
      { ul: ['Unit tests are organised into a universal test suite and run **daily** (regression).', '**Acceptance tests** (customer tests) are specified by the customer from the user stories and check overall features visible to the customer.'] },
      { h: 'Industrial XP (IXP)' },
      'An organic evolution of XP for large organisations, adding six practices: readiness assessment, project community, project chartering, test-driven management, retrospectives and continuous learning.',
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Advantages', items: ['Fast, frequent delivery of working software', 'Early defect detection through test-first and pairs', 'Customer involvement throughout', 'Simple, refactored code is easy to change'] },
        { title: 'Disadvantages', items: ['Needs an on-site customer', 'Little documentation; hard for large or distributed teams', 'Pair programming can seem costly', 'Requirements volatility can cause rework'] },
      ] },
    ],
    terms: [['User story', 'Customer-written card describing a feature.'], ['Project velocity', 'Number of stories delivered in the last release.'], ['Spike solution', 'Operational prototype of a hard design problem.'], ['Refactoring', 'Improving code structure without changing behaviour.'], ['Pair programming', 'Two developers at one workstation.']],
    exam: 'Define XP, draw the XP process, explain the four activities with their practices (user stories, velocity, KIS, CRC, spike, refactoring, test-first, pair programming, continuous integration, acceptance tests), and list the values.',
    revise: ['Planning, design, coding, testing', 'Stories, velocity, KIS, CRC, spike, refactor, test-first, pairs, CI', 'Values: communication, simplicity, feedback, courage, respect'],
  },

  'se.u2.asd': {
    oneLine: 'Adaptive Software Development (ASD), proposed by Jim Highsmith, builds complex systems with self-organising teams through repeated cycles of speculation, collaboration and learning.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Adaptive Software Development (ASD)', text: 'An agile technique proposed by **Jim Highsmith** for building complex software and systems. Its philosophy focuses on **human collaboration and team self-organisation**. The ASD life cycle has three phases: **speculation, collaboration and learning**.' },
      { img: 'se-asd' },
      { table: { head: ['Phase', 'What happens'], rows: [
        ['**Speculation**', 'The project is initiated and **adaptive cycle planning** is conducted using the customer\'s mission statement, project constraints (delivery dates, user descriptions) and basic requirements to define the set of **release cycles** (increments). "Speculation" replaces "planning" because the plan is expected to change.'],
        ['**Collaboration**', 'Motivated people work together in a way that multiplies their talent and creativity. Collaboration needs **trust**: criticise without animosity, assist without resentment, work as hard as possible, have the skills, and communicate problems. Requirements gathering uses JAD sessions and mini-specs.'],
        ['**Learning**', 'As members build components, the emphasis is on learning as much as on progress. Three ways: **focus groups** (customers and users give feedback on increments), **formal technical reviews** (team reviews components), and **postmortems** (the team reflects on its own performance and process).'],
      ] } },
      'ASD\'s overall emphasis on the dynamics of **self-organising teams**, interpersonal collaboration and individual and team learning yields teams that have a much higher likelihood of success.',
      { h: 'Why ASD teams succeed more often' },
      { ul: ['Self-organising teams share responsibility, so problems are spotted early.', 'Collaboration builds trust and better ideas.', 'Short cycles and reviews mean mistakes are learned from quickly.', 'Plans are adapted rather than defended.'] },
    ],
    terms: [['Speculation', 'ASD planning that expects change.'], ['Focus group', 'Users giving feedback on an increment.'], ['Postmortem', 'Team reflection on its own process.']],
    exam: 'Define ASD (Highsmith), draw the speculation–collaboration–learning cycle, explain each phase in detail, and say why ASD teams succeed.',
    revise: ['Speculation → collaboration → learning', 'Learning: focus groups, technical reviews, postmortems', 'Self-organising teams'],
  },

  'se.u2.scrum': {
    oneLine: 'Scrum is an agile process in which work is pulled from a prioritised product backlog into sprint backlogs and done in short, time-boxed sprints (typically 30 days or less) with a daily 15-minute Scrum meeting, ending in a demo of a potentially shippable increment.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Scrum', text: 'An agile process model (named after a rugby formation, developed by Jeff Sutherland and Ken Schwaber) whose principles guide development activities within a process that incorporates **requirements, analysis, design, evolution and delivery**. Within each framework activity, work tasks occur within a process pattern called a **sprint**.' },
      { img: 'se-scrum' },
      { h: 'Scrum artefacts and events' },
      { table: { head: ['Element', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**Product backlog**', 'A **prioritised list of project requirements or features** that provide business value for the customer. Items can be added at any time (that is how changes are introduced); the **product owner** assesses and updates priorities.'],
        ['**Sprint**', 'A **time-boxed** work unit (usually 2–4 weeks, at most 30 days) needed to achieve a requirement defined in the backlog. **Changes are not introduced during the sprint**, so team members work in a short-term but stable environment.'],
        ['**Sprint backlog**', 'The subset of backlog items selected for the current sprint, broken into tasks'],
        ['**Scrum meeting (daily scrum)**', 'A short (typically **15-minute**) meeting held daily. Each member answers: What did you do since the last meeting? What obstacles are you encountering? What do you plan to accomplish by the next meeting?'],
        ['**Demo (sprint review)**', 'Delivers the software increment to the customer so the implemented functionality can be **demonstrated and evaluated**'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Roles' },
      { table: { head: ['Role', 'Responsibility'], rows: [['Product owner', 'Represents the customer; owns and prioritises the product backlog'], ['Scrum master', 'Leads the meetings, assesses responses, removes obstacles (impediments)'], ['Development team', 'Self-organising, cross-functional; builds the increment']] } },
      { h: 'Importance of backlogs and sprints' },
      { ul: ['The **backlog** is the single source of all requirements, **prioritised by business value**, so the most valuable features are built first; it absorbs **changing requirements** without disturbing current work.', '**Sprints** give a short, fixed time-box with a clear goal, produce a **working increment** every few weeks, enable regular customer feedback, and make progress visible and predictable.'] },
      { h: 'Choosing an agile model for short iterations with continuous customer feedback' },
      '**Scrum** is the most suitable: work is done in **short time-boxed sprints**; every sprint ends with a **demo to the customer**, whose feedback goes straight into the **re-prioritised product backlog** for the next sprint; daily scrums surface problems early; and the product owner represents the customer continuously. (XP is also iterative with an on-site customer, but Scrum provides the explicit framework of sprints, backlog and reviews for managing iterations.)',
      { tip: 'Class Test I asked "the importance of backlogs and sprints" (2 marks) and "a suitable agile model for short iterations with continuous customer feedback" (3 marks). For the second, name Scrum, draw the flow and justify with sprint, backlog, demo and daily scrum.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Product backlog', 'Prioritised list of requirements.'], ['Sprint', 'Time-boxed iteration, at most 30 days.'], ['Daily scrum', '15-minute daily meeting with three questions.'], ['Scrum master', 'Leads meetings and removes impediments.']],
    exam: 'Define Scrum, draw the Scrum flow, explain the backlog, sprint, sprint backlog, daily scrum and demo, list the roles, and explain the importance of backlogs and sprints.',
    revise: ['Backlog → sprint backlog → sprint (daily scrum) → demo', 'No changes during a sprint', 'Roles: product owner, Scrum master, team'],
  },

  'se.u2.dsdm': {
    oneLine: 'The Dynamic Systems Development Method (DSDM) is an agile approach that provides a framework for building and maintaining systems which meet tight time constraints through incremental prototyping, guided by the Pareto principle (80% of the value in 20% of the time).',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'DSDM', text: 'The **Dynamic Systems Development Method** is an agile software development approach that "provides a framework for building and maintaining systems which meet tight time constraints through the use of **incremental prototyping** in a controlled project environment". It is maintained by the **DSDM Consortium**.' },
      'DSDM borrows a modified version of the **Pareto principle**: **80% of an application can be delivered in 20% of the time** it would take to deliver the complete (100%) application. Each iteration delivers only enough functionality to move to the next increment; the remaining detail can be completed later when business requirements are known or changes have been accommodated.',
      { img: 'se-dsdm' },
      { table: { head: ['Activity', 'Purpose'], rows: [
        ['**Feasibility study**', 'Establishes the basic business requirements and constraints, and assesses whether the application is a viable candidate for DSDM'],
        ['**Business study**', 'Establishes the functional and information requirements that let the application provide business value; defines the basic architecture and maintainability requirements'],
        ['**Functional model iteration**', 'Produces a set of **incremental prototypes** that demonstrate functionality; gathers additional requirements from users as they exercise the prototype'],
        ['**Design and build iteration**', 'Revisits prototypes built during functional model iteration to ensure each has been engineered to provide operational business value'],
        ['**Implementation**', 'Places the latest software increment into the operational environment'],
      ] } },
      'Functional model iteration and design and build iteration can occur **concurrently**. DSDM uses **time-boxing** and **MoSCoW** prioritisation (must have, should have, could have, won\'t have this time), and can be combined with XP.',
    ],
    terms: [['Pareto principle', '80% of the value from 20% of the effort.'], ['Time box', 'Fixed time for an increment; scope is cut, not the deadline.'], ['MoSCoW', 'Must, should, could, won\'t prioritisation.']],
    exam: 'Define DSDM, state the 80/20 principle, draw the life cycle and explain the five activities, and mention time-boxing and MoSCoW.',
    revise: ['80/20; incremental prototyping; time boxes', 'Feasibility, business study, functional model iteration, design and build iteration, implementation'],
  },

  'se.u2.fdd': {
    oneLine: 'Feature Driven Development (FDD), by Peter Coad, is an agile, object-oriented process that plans, designs and builds a system one small client-valued feature at a time, each deliverable in two weeks or less.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Feature Driven Development (FDD)', text: 'An adaptive, agile process originally conceived by **Peter Coad** for object-oriented software engineering. It emphasises collaboration, manages problem and project complexity using **feature-based decomposition** followed by integration of software increments, and communicates technical detail with verbal, graphical and text-based means.' },
      { def: 'Feature', text: 'A **client-valued function that can be implemented in two weeks or less**. Template: **<action> the <result> <by | for | of | to> a(n) <object>**, e.g. "Add the product to a shopping cart", "Display the technical specifications of a product", "Store the shipping information for a customer". A **feature set** groups related features (e.g. "Making a product sale").' },
      { img: 'se-fdd' },
      { table: { head: ['Activity', 'What happens'], rows: [['Develop an overall model', 'Domain walkthroughs; an object model of the whole system'], ['Build a features list', 'Decompose the domain into subject areas, business activities and features'], ['Plan by feature', 'Sequence feature sets; assign classes to owners'], ['Design by feature', 'Design packages and sequence diagrams for a group of features; design inspection'], ['Build by feature', 'Code, unit test, code inspection, promote to the build']] } },
      { h: 'Benefits of the feature emphasis' },
      { ul: ['Features are small blocks of deliverable functionality, so users can describe them easily and review them.', 'Features can be organised into a hierarchical business grouping.', 'A feature is the deliverable increment, so the team develops operational features every two weeks.', 'Design and code representations are inspected per feature.', 'Project planning, scheduling and tracking are driven by the feature hierarchy; progress is reported by milestones per feature.', 'Suits **large teams** and business systems with many well-defined functions (banking, inventory, e-commerce).'] },
    ],
    terms: [['Feature', 'Client-valued function deliverable in ≤ 2 weeks.'], ['Feature set', 'Group of related features.'], ['Class owner', 'Developer responsible for a class.']],
    exam: 'Define FDD and a feature (with the template and examples), draw the five activities and explain each, and list the benefits of the feature emphasis.',
    revise: ['Feature = client value, ≤ 2 weeks', 'Model → features list → plan → design → build by feature'],
  },

  'se.u2.am': {
    oneLine: 'Agile Modelling (AM), proposed by Scott Ambler, is a practice-based methodology for effective modelling and documentation: model with a purpose, use multiple models, travel light, value content over representation, know your models and tools, and adapt locally.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Agile Modelling (AM)', text: 'A **practice-based methodology for effective modelling and documentation** of software systems (Scott Ambler). It is a collection of values, principles and practices for modelling software that can be applied in a light and effective way: agile models are **"just barely good enough"**.' },
      'Large, business-critical systems still need models to understand and communicate, but agile teams avoid heavy, unused documentation. AM adopts all the values of the Agile Manifesto and adds **courage** (to make decisions and to change direction) and **humility** (recognising that others may know more).',
      { h: 'Principles of Agile Modelling' },
      { table: { head: ['Principle', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**Model with a purpose**', 'Have a specific goal (e.g. communicate information to the customer, understand a part of the software) before creating a model'],
        ['**Use multiple models**', 'Many models and notations exist; each gives a different view; use only those that add value'],
        ['**Travel light**', 'Keep only the models that will provide long-term value; discard the rest as work proceeds'],
        ['**Content is more important than representation**', 'A model that conveys information to the audience is useful even if its notation is imperfect (a whiteboard sketch can be enough)'],
        ['**Know the models and the tools**', 'Understand the strengths and weaknesses of each model and the tools used to build it'],
        ['**Adapt locally**', 'Adapt the modelling approach to the needs of the agile team'],
      ] } },
      'Other practices: model in small increments, active stakeholder participation, collective ownership, create several models in parallel, apply the right artefacts, iterate to another artefact, prove it with code, and display models publicly.',
    ],
    terms: [['Agile modelling', 'Lightweight, value-driven modelling practices.'], ['Travel light', 'Keep only models of lasting value.'], ['Barely good enough', 'Models just sufficient for their purpose.']],
    exam: 'Define Agile Modelling (Ambler), state why models are still needed, and explain its principles with one line each.',
    revise: ['Purpose, multiple models, travel light, content over representation, know tools, adapt locally'],
  },

  'se.u2.req-engineering': {
    oneLine: 'Requirements engineering is the broad spectrum of tasks and techniques that lead to an understanding of requirements: inception, elicitation, elaboration, negotiation, specification, validation and requirements management; it separates functional from non-functional requirements and user from system requirements.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Requirements engineering', text: 'The **broad spectrum of tasks and techniques that lead to an understanding of requirements**. It builds a bridge to design and construction: it establishes what the customer wants, analyses need, assesses feasibility, negotiates a reasonable solution, specifies it unambiguously, validates the specification and manages requirements as they are transformed into an operational system.' },
      { def: 'Requirement', text: 'A **statement of a service the system must provide or a constraint under which it must operate**. A **functional requirement** describes what the system should do; a **non-functional requirement** describes a quality or constraint (how well).' },
      { img: 'se-req-tasks' },
      { table: { head: ['Task', 'What happens'], rows: [
        ['**Inception**', 'Establish a basic understanding of the problem, the people who want a solution, the nature of the solution desired, and the effectiveness of communication'],
        ['**Elicitation**', 'Ask the customer, users and others what the objectives are, what is to be accomplished, how the system fits business needs and how it will be used. Problems: scope, understanding, volatility'],
        ['**Elaboration**', 'Expand and refine the information into a refined requirements model: user scenarios, analysis classes, attributes, services, relationships'],
        ['**Negotiation**', 'Reconcile conflicts: rank requirements by priority, discuss them, assess cost and risk, iterate until stakeholders are satisfied'],
        ['**Specification**', 'A written document, a set of graphical models, a formal mathematical model, a collection of usage scenarios, a prototype, or any combination (the SRS)'],
        ['**Validation**', 'Examine the specification to ensure all requirements are stated unambiguously, inconsistencies, omissions and errors are corrected, and work products conform to standards (technical review)'],
        ['**Requirements management**', 'Activities that help the team identify, control and track requirements and changes to them as the project proceeds (traceability tables)'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Functional vs non-functional requirements' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Functional', items: ['What the system must **do**: services, inputs, outputs, behaviour', '"A student shall be able to submit feedback for each course."', 'Tested by functional/black-box tests'] },
        { title: 'Non-functional', items: ['How **well** it does it: performance, security, usability, reliability, portability', '"Feedback pages load in under 2 seconds."', 'Constrain the whole system'] },
      ] },
      'User requirements are written in natural language and diagrams for customers; system requirements are detailed, technical descriptions for developers.',
      { h: 'Applying requirements engineering: an Online Student Feedback System' },
      { table: { head: ['Step', 'How it is done for the feedback system'], rows: [
        ['**Elicitation**', 'Identify stakeholders (students, faculty, HODs, IQAC/admin, IT staff). Hold **collaborative requirements-gathering** meetings with a facilitator, interview faculty and admin, send a **questionnaire** to students, study existing paper forms, write **usage scenarios** ("a student logs in, sees pending courses, rates each on a 1–5 scale, submits anonymously"). Use **QFD**: normal (online forms, reports), expected (anonymity, mobile access), exciting (sentiment summary of comments)'],
        ['**Negotiation**', 'Resolve conflicts with Boehm\'s **win-win** approach: students want **anonymity** but admin wants to stop duplicate or abusive entries → win-win: authenticated login, but responses stored without identity; faculty want to see raw comments vs students\' privacy → show only aggregated scores and filtered comments; prioritise features within budget and the semester deadline (must-have: forms, anonymity, reports)'],
        ['**Validation**', 'Hold a **formal technical review** of the SRS with representatives of every stakeholder group; check each requirement is consistent, unambiguous ("anonymous" defined precisely), complete, feasible and **testable**; build a **prototype** of the feedback form for students to try; write test cases from requirements; record defects and revise the SRS'],
      ] } },
      { tip: 'Class Test I question 6(b) (4 marks): give one paragraph each on elicitation, negotiation and validation, written in terms of the feedback system, as in the table above.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Requirements engineering', 'Tasks leading to an understanding and specification of requirements.'], ['Functional requirement', 'What the system must do.'], ['Non-functional requirement', 'A quality or constraint.'], ['Traceability table', 'Links requirements to sources, design and tests.']],
    exam: 'Define requirements engineering, draw and explain its seven tasks, distinguish functional and non-functional (and user vs system) requirements, and apply elicitation, negotiation and validation to a given system.',
    revise: ['Inception, elicitation, elaboration, negotiation, specification, validation, management', 'Functional = what; non-functional = how well'],
  },

  'se.u2.groundwork': {
    oneLine: 'Establishing the groundwork prepares for requirements engineering: identify the stakeholders, recognise their multiple viewpoints, work toward collaboration, and ask the first (context-free) questions.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Establishing the groundwork', text: 'The steps required to **establish the foundation for an understanding of software requirements**: to get the project started in a way that will keep it moving toward a successful solution, even when stakeholders are in different places, have limited time, or disagree.' },
      { flow: ['Identify the **stakeholders**', 'Recognise **multiple viewpoints**', 'Work toward **collaboration**', 'Ask the **first questions**'] },
      { ul: [
        '**Identifying stakeholders**: a stakeholder is "anyone who benefits in a direct or indirect way from the system being developed": business managers, product managers, marketing, internal and external customers, end users, consultants, product engineers, software engineers, support and maintenance engineers. Create a list, then ask each stakeholder "Whom else do you think I should talk to?"',
        '**Recognising multiple viewpoints**: marketing wants features that sell, business managers want features within budget and schedule, end users want ease of use, engineers want infrastructure support, support staff want maintainability. Collect all views, including inconsistent and conflicting ones.',
        '**Working toward collaboration**: the requirements engineer finds areas of commonality and areas of conflict or inconsistency; collaboration does not mean decisions by committee: a "project champion" (business manager or senior technologist) may make the final decision.',
      ] },
      { h: 'Asking the first questions' },
      { table: { head: ['Set', 'Example questions'], rows: [
        ['Context-free: stakeholders, goals, benefits', 'Who is behind the request for this work? Who will use the solution? What will be the economic benefit of a successful solution? Is there another source for the solution?'],
        ['Understanding the problem and perceived solution', 'How would you characterise "good" output? What problems will this solution address? Can you describe the business environment? Will special performance issues or constraints affect the solution?'],
        ['Meta-questions (effectiveness of communication)', 'Are you the right person to answer these questions? Are your answers "official"? Are my questions relevant? Am I asking too many? Can anyone else provide information? Should I be asking you anything else?'],
      ] } },
    ],
    terms: [['Stakeholder', 'Anyone who benefits directly or indirectly from the system.'], ['Context-free question', 'Question that does not presuppose the solution.'], ['Meta-question', 'Question about the questioning process itself.']],
    exam: 'Explain the four groundwork steps (stakeholders, viewpoints, collaboration, first questions) with examples, and give the three sets of first questions.',
    revise: ['Stakeholders → viewpoints → collaboration → first questions', 'Context-free, problem, meta-questions'],
  },

  'se.u2.eliciting': {
    oneLine: 'Eliciting requirements draws requirements out of stakeholders through collaborative requirements gathering, quality function deployment (normal, expected, exciting requirements), usage scenarios and agreed work products.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Requirements elicitation', text: 'The activity (also called **requirements gathering**) that **combines elements of problem solving, elaboration, negotiation and specification** to identify the problem, propose elements of the solution, negotiate different approaches and specify a preliminary set of requirements, in a collaborative, team-oriented way.' },
      { h: '1. Collaborative requirements gathering' },
      { ul: ['Meetings are conducted and attended by **both software engineers and other stakeholders**.', '**Rules for preparation and participation** are established.', 'An **agenda** is suggested that is formal enough to cover all important points but informal enough to encourage the free flow of ideas.', 'A **facilitator** (customer, developer or outsider) controls the meeting.', 'A **definition mechanism** (work sheets, flip charts, wall stickers, electronic bulletin board, chat room, virtual forum) is used.', 'Goal: identify the problem, propose elements of the solution, negotiate approaches and specify a preliminary set of solution requirements.'] },
      'Before the meeting, each attendee makes lists of objects, services, constraints and performance criteria; in the meeting the lists are combined, discussed and refined into **mini-specifications**.',
      { h: '2. Quality Function Deployment (QFD)' },
      { def: 'Quality Function Deployment', text: 'A quality management technique that **translates the needs of the customer into technical requirements** for software, concentrating on maximising customer satisfaction.' },
      { table: { head: ['Type', 'Meaning', 'Example (mobile phone)'], rows: [['**Normal requirements**', 'Objectives and goals stated during meetings; if present, the customer is satisfied', 'Specific graphical displays, functions'], ['**Expected requirements**', 'Implicit; so fundamental the customer does not state them, but their absence causes dissatisfaction', 'Ease of human–machine interaction, ease of installation, reliability'], ['**Exciting requirements**', 'Go beyond expectations; very satisfying when present', 'Multitouch screen, visual voice mail']] } },
      'QFD uses customer interviews, observation, surveys and historical data; it produces a **customer voice table** reviewed with the customer.',
      { h: '3. Usage scenarios' },
      'Developers and users create **scenarios (use cases)** that identify a thread of usage for the system to be constructed, describing how the system will be used.',
      { h: '4. Elicitation work products' },
      { ul: ['A statement of need and feasibility', 'A bounded statement of scope', 'A list of customers, users and other stakeholders who participated', 'A description of the system\'s technical environment', 'A list of requirements (organised by function) and the domain constraints on each', 'A set of usage scenarios', 'Any prototypes developed to define requirements'] },
      { warn: 'Problems in elicitation: **scope** (boundary ill-defined, unnecessary technical detail), **understanding** (customers unsure of needs, poor understanding of the computing environment, ambiguous or untestable requirements), **volatility** (requirements change over time).' },
    ],
    terms: [['Facilitator', 'Runs a requirements-gathering meeting.'], ['QFD', 'Translates customer needs into technical requirements.'], ['Mini-specification', 'Refined description of a listed item.'], ['Usage scenario', 'Thread of usage describing how the system will be used.']],
    exam: 'Define elicitation, list the guidelines of collaborative requirements gathering, explain QFD with normal, expected and exciting requirements and examples, describe usage scenarios, and list the work products and problems.',
    revise: ['Collaborative meeting: rules, agenda, facilitator, definition mechanism', 'QFD: normal, expected, exciting', 'Problems: scope, understanding, volatility'],
  },

  'se.u2.negotiating': {
    oneLine: 'Negotiating requirements reconciles conflicts between stakeholders and between wishes and resources; Boehm\'s win-win approach identifies key stakeholders, determines their win conditions and negotiates them into a set of win-win conditions.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Negotiating requirements', text: 'The process of **reconciling conflicts** when different customers or users propose conflicting requirements, or ask for more than can be achieved with limited resources, so that **each party achieves some measure of satisfaction**. The ideal is a **"win-win" result**: stakeholders get a system that satisfies most of their needs and the team works to realistic budgets and deadlines.' },
      { h: "Boehm's win-win negotiation activities" },
      { steps: ['**Identify the system or subsystem\'s key stakeholders.**', '**Determine each stakeholder\'s "win conditions"**: what they need to be satisfied.', '**Negotiate the stakeholders\' win conditions** to reconcile them into a set of **win-win conditions** for all concerned (including the software team).'] },
      'Negotiation also ranks requirements by priority, assesses their cost and risk, and addresses internal conflicts; requirements may be eliminated, combined or modified.',
      { h: 'The art of negotiation (guidelines)' },
      { ol: ['**Recognise that it is not a competition**: both sides must feel they have won.', '**Map out a strategy**: decide what you would like, what the other party wants, and how you will make both happen.', '**Listen actively**.', '**Focus on the other party\'s interests**, not their positions.', '**Do not let it get personal**: focus on the problem.', '**Be creative** when at an impasse.', '**Be ready to commit** once an agreement has been reached.'] },
      { example: 'Online student feedback: students want full anonymity, administrators want to prevent duplicate submissions. Win-win: students log in (one response per course is enforced), but responses are stored without identity, so both win conditions are met.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Win condition', 'What a stakeholder needs to be satisfied.'], ['Win-win', 'Outcome acceptable to all stakeholders.']],
    exam: 'Define negotiation and why it is needed, explain Boehm\'s three win-win activities, list the negotiation guidelines, and give an example.',
    revise: ['Key stakeholders → win conditions → win-win conditions', 'Not a competition; listen; interests not positions; commit'],
  },

  'se.u2.validating': {
    oneLine: 'Validating requirements examines the requirements model for consistency, omissions and ambiguity before design begins, mainly by technical reviews with a checklist of questions, prototyping and test-case generation.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Requirements validation', text: 'The process of **examining the requirements specification to ensure that all software requirements have been stated unambiguously; that inconsistencies, omissions and errors have been detected and corrected; and that the work products conform to the standards** established for the process, project and product. The primary mechanism is the **technical review**.' },
      { h: 'Checklist questions for validation' },
      { ul: ['Is each requirement **consistent** with the overall objectives for the system/product?', 'Have all requirements been specified at the proper **level of abstraction**?', 'Is the requirement really **necessary**, or is it an add-on feature?', 'Is each requirement **bounded and unambiguous**?', 'Does each requirement have **attribution** (a source)?', 'Do any requirements **conflict** with other requirements?', 'Is each requirement **achievable** in the technical environment?', 'Is each requirement **testable** once implemented?', 'Does the model reflect the information, function and behaviour of the system?', 'Has the model been partitioned to expose progressively more detailed information?', 'Have requirements patterns been used and validated?'] },
      { h: 'Validation techniques' },
      { ul: ['**Requirements reviews / inspections**: stakeholders examine the SRS systematically.', '**Prototyping**: an executable model shown to users to check that requirements reflect their needs.', '**Test-case generation**: if tests are hard to design, the requirement is likely unclear.', '**Consistency (automated) analysis** of models.'] },
      { flow: ['Plan the review', 'Distribute the SRS to reviewers', 'Individual preparation', 'Review meeting', 'Record errors and issues', 'Agree actions', 'Revise and re-review'], label: 'Requirements review process' },
      'Participants: requirements engineer, customer and user representatives, designer, tester and a moderator. When an error is found, the owner corrects it, the change is recorded under requirements management, and the corrected part is re-reviewed.',
    ],
    terms: [['Requirements validation', 'Checking that requirements correctly describe the customer\'s needs.'], ['Technical review', 'Formal examination of work products to find errors.'], ['Attribution', 'The known source of a requirement.']],
    exam: 'Define validation, list the checklist questions, describe the validation techniques and the review process.',
    revise: ['Consistent, necessary, unambiguous, attributed, non-conflicting, achievable, testable', 'Reviews, prototyping, test cases'],
  },

  'se.u2.srs-value': {
    oneLine: 'The Software Requirements Specification is the document that bridges the client\'s needs and the developers; a good SRS establishes the agreement between client and developer, reduces development cost, and is the basis for estimates, validation and verification.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Software Requirements Specification (SRS)', text: 'A document that **completely describes what the proposed software should do without describing how** the software will do it. It is the medium through which the client\'s and user\'s needs are accurately specified to the developer, and the basis of the **agreement (contract) between client and supplier**.' },
      { h: 'Value (need) of a good SRS' },
      { ol: ['**Establishes the basis for agreement** between the client and the supplier on what the software product will do.', '**Provides a reference for validation** of the final product: acceptance tests are derived from it.', '**A high-quality SRS is a prerequisite to high-quality software**: errors in requirements propagate to design and code.', '**Reduces development cost**: the cost of fixing a requirement error grows sharply in later phases (it can be 100 times more expensive after delivery), so a good SRS minimises changes and rework.', 'Provides a basis for **estimating** cost and schedule and for planning.', 'Helps the client understand their own needs and makes **enhancement** easier.'] },
      { h: 'Characteristics of a good SRS (IEEE 830)' },
      { table: { head: ['Characteristic', 'Meaning'], rows: [['**Correct**', 'Every requirement stated is one the software must meet'], ['**Complete**', 'Everything the software must do, and its responses to all classes of input (valid and invalid), is specified'], ['**Unambiguous**', 'Every requirement has only one interpretation'], ['**Consistent**', 'No subset of requirements conflicts'], ['**Verifiable**', 'There is a cost-effective process to check each requirement'], ['**Ranked for importance and/or stability**', 'Requirements are prioritised (critical, important, desirable)'], ['**Modifiable**', 'Its structure allows changes easily and consistently'], ['**Traceable**', 'The origin of each requirement is clear, and it can be referenced in later documents (backward and forward traceability)']] } },
      'Of these, **completeness** is the hardest to achieve, and **verifiability** and **unambiguity** are the most important for testing.',
    ],
    terms: [['SRS', 'Document specifying what the software must do.'], ['Verifiable', 'Can be checked cost-effectively.'], ['Traceable', 'Origin and later references are clear.']],
    exam: 'Define the SRS, explain why a good SRS is valuable (agreement, validation, quality, cost reduction), and list and explain the characteristics of a good SRS.',
    revise: ['Agreement, validation basis, quality, cost reduction', 'Correct, complete, unambiguous, consistent, verifiable, ranked, modifiable, traceable'],
  },

  'se.u2.problem-analysis': {
    oneLine: 'Problem analysis aims to obtain a clear understanding of the needs of the clients and users and of the problem domain, guided by partitioning, abstraction and projection; approaches include the informal approach, data flow modelling with DFDs and data dictionaries, object-oriented modelling and prototyping.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Problem analysis', text: 'The activity whose aim is to **obtain a clear understanding of the needs of the clients and the users, what exactly is desired from the software, and what the constraints on the solution are**. It deals with the **problem domain** (not the solution) and produces the understanding that is then written as the SRS.' },
      { h: 'Basic principles' },
      { ul: ['**Partitioning** (divide and conquer): break the problem into smaller sub-problems and understand each and their relationships.', '**Abstraction**: understand a component by its external behaviour, ignoring its internal details.', '**Projection**: look at the system from different viewpoints (users, operators, maintainers) and combine them.'] },
      { h: 'Approaches' },
      { table: { head: ['Approach', 'Description'], rows: [
        ['**Informal approach**', 'No defined methodology: the analyst talks to clients and users (meetings, questionnaires, documents) and writes the understanding directly as the SRS, refined through reviews'],
        ['**Data flow modelling (structured analysis)**', 'The system is viewed as a function that transforms inputs into outputs through processes; modelled with **data flow diagrams** (context diagram, then levelled DFDs) and a **data dictionary** that defines every data flow and store'],
        ['**Object-oriented modelling**', 'The system is viewed as a set of interacting **objects**: identify classes, their attributes, services (operations), and relationships (inheritance, aggregation, association); use UML class and use-case diagrams'],
        ['**Prototyping**', 'Build a partial system so that clients and users can understand and refine their needs; **throwaway** or **evolutionary**'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Data dictionary example' },
      { code: `weekly_timesheet = employee_name + employee_id + {regular_hours + overtime_hours}
pay_rate         = [hourly | daily | weekly] + dollar_amount
employee_name    = last + first + middle_initial` },
      'Notation: + composition, {} repetition, [ | ] selection, () optional.',
    ],
    terms: [['Partitioning', 'Breaking a problem into sub-problems.'], ['Abstraction', 'Viewing a component by its external behaviour.'], ['Projection', 'Viewing the system from different perspectives.'], ['Data dictionary', 'Definitions of all data in the DFDs.']],
    exam: 'Define problem analysis and its aim, explain partitioning, abstraction and projection, and describe the four approaches (informal, data flow with DFD and data dictionary, object-oriented, prototyping).',
    revise: ['Aim: understand the problem domain and needs', 'Partitioning, abstraction, projection', 'Informal, data flow, OO, prototyping'],
  },

  'se.u2.req-spec': {
    oneLine: 'Requirements specification writes the analysed requirements as an SRS: it covers functionality, performance, design constraints and external interfaces, can use natural, structured, graphical or formal languages, and follows a standard structure such as IEEE 830.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Requirements specification', text: 'The final output of requirements activities: the **SRS document**, which **specifies the requirements identified during analysis** in a form that is precise, complete and verifiable. Analysis focuses on understanding; specification focuses on **what to write and how**, so the document has the characteristics of a good SRS.' },
      { h: 'Components of an SRS' },
      { table: { head: ['Component', 'What it specifies'], rows: [
        ['**Functionality**', 'All functions the software must support: input, processing and output for each, including behaviour for invalid inputs and abnormal situations'],
        ['**Performance requirements**', 'Static (number of terminals, users, files) and dynamic (response time, throughput) constraints, stated in measurable terms'],
        ['**Design constraints**', 'Standards compliance, hardware limitations, reliability and fault tolerance, security'],
        ['**External interfaces**', 'User interfaces, hardware interfaces, software interfaces, communication interfaces'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Specification language' },
      { ul: ['**Natural language**: easy to read, but imprecise and ambiguous; use structured, standardised phrases ("The system shall…").', '**Structured / tabular**: decision tables, structured English.', '**Graphical**: DFDs, use-case and state diagrams.', '**Formal**: Z, VDM: precise and verifiable, harder to read.'] },
      { h: 'Structure of an SRS (IEEE 830)' },
      { stack: ['**1. Introduction**: purpose, scope, definitions/acronyms, references, overview', '**2. Overall description**: product perspective, product functions, user characteristics, general constraints, assumptions and dependencies', '**3. Specific requirements**: external interface requirements, functional requirements, performance requirements, design constraints, attributes (security, maintainability), other requirements', '**Appendices and index**'], label: 'IEEE 830 SRS outline' },
      { h: 'Use cases' },
      'A **use case** captures a contract between the system and its users about behaviour: actors, a main success scenario and extensions (exceptional scenarios). Use cases are a good way to specify functional requirements.',
      { example: 'Railway reservation. Functional: search trains between stations on a date; book a ticket and generate a PNR; cancel and refund; check PNR status. Non-functional: support 10,000 concurrent users; booking response under 3 s; secure payment over HTTPS; 99.9% availability.' },
    ],
    terms: [['IEEE 830', 'IEEE recommended practice for SRS.'], ['Use case', 'Contract of behaviour between system and actors.'], ['Design constraint', 'Standard, hardware or security limit on the solution.']],
    exam: 'Define requirements specification, list the components of an SRS (functionality, performance, design constraints, interfaces), discuss specification languages, give the IEEE 830 structure, and write functional and non-functional requirements for a given system.',
    revise: ['Functionality, performance, constraints, interfaces', 'Natural, structured, graphical, formal', 'IEEE 830: introduction, overall description, specific requirements'],
  },
}
